UNIT adopt_find_duplicate
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@7b348e351c9f cli/lib/adopt.mts findUnmanagedDuplicate (with its helpers sectionSignature, stripMarkers, findHeadings, linesOf, isFence, isOneLineFence, fenceRun, closesFence, normalizeHeadingText) — the code is the truth
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG adopt_find_duplicate(existing: text, section: text) -> record[heading: text, startLine: i64, endLine: i64] | none
ENVELOPE any two texts of well-formed Unicode (no lone surrogates). Every such pair has a result; nothing throws.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION During `rdv init --adopt`: locate the block of an existing CLAUDE.md (existing) that is an unmanaged copy of
  a profile's section (section), or none. The result names the block's first heading line (heading: that line, R1 form,
  with WHITESPACE trimmed from both ends) and the half-open line range [startLine, endLine) it spans, lines counted from 0.
  The block is a contiguous run of sections of existing whose headings match the profile's, confirmed by profile lines
  found inside the run.
BEHAVIOR
R1 LINES. A text's lines are the pieces between LF characters (n LFs give n + 1 lines; a final LF gives an empty last
  line; empty text is one empty line), each with ONE trailing CR removed when it ends in CR. Any other CR stays in the line
  ("x" CR CR LF leaves "x" CR).
R2 FENCES. A line is a ONE-LINE FENCE when, WHITESPACE-trimmed at both ends, it begins with three backticks, ends with three
  backticks, is at least 7 UTF-16 units long, and contains no BREAK-CHAR ("```x```", "```````" and "``` ```" are; "``````",
  "```x``` y" and "```x" U+2028 "y```" are not). A line is a FENCE line when, after its leading WHITESPACE, it begins with
  three backticks or three tildes (longer runs and any following text included) and it is NOT a one-line fence ("~~~x~~~" is
  a fence line). A line's RUN is the whole run of backticks or of tildes that starts right after its leading WHITESPACE, when
  that run has at least 3 characters (else it has no run). Reading the lines in order, starting outside a fence: outside, a
  fence line OPENS a fence and is its OPENER (a one-line fence opens nothing); inside, a line CLOSES the fence exactly when
  it has a run of the opener's run character, at least as long as the opener's run, and nothing but WHITESPACE after the run
  (CITED FIDELITY package: CommonMark's closing rule; indentation and trailing whitespace are any WHITESPACE). Every other
  line inside is content, fence lines included ("```" inside a "~~~" fence, "```" inside "````", "```bash" inside "```" close
  nothing); an unclosed fence runs to the end of the text. The opener, the closer and every line between are never headings.
R3 HEADING. Any other line is a heading when it begins with a run of 1 to 6 "#" (the run is the whole leading run of "#";
  seven or more -> not a heading) and the rest T after that run (a) begins with a WHITESPACE character, (b) has at least 2
  characters, and (c) every BREAK-CHAR character in T lies inside T's leading WHITESPACE run and is not T's last character.
  "#x", "# " and "####### x" are not headings; "#  " (two spaces) is a heading (with an empty key).
R4 HEADING-KEY of a heading's T: (1) if T, after dropping trailing WHITESPACE, ends with ")", and a "(" occurs after the
  previous ")" (or the start) and before that final ")", cut T at the FIRST such "(" (the trailing parenthetical and
  everything after it go); (2) lowercase with the full Unicode lowercase mapping; (3) every maximal run of characters
  other than ASCII a-z and 0-9 becomes one separator; the key is the remaining runs joined by single spaces, with no
  leading or trailing space (it may be empty). Step (2) yields ASCII letters only from A-Z, U+212A KELVIN SIGN (-> "k")
  and U+0130 (-> "i" then a separator); every other non-ASCII character becomes a separator.
  ("Hard rules (a) (b)" -> "hard rules a"; "Hard rules (a (b)" -> "hard rules"; "(draft)" and "---" -> "").
R5 NORMALIZED line: every maximal run of WHITESPACE becomes one space, then a leading and trailing space are removed.
  Length is counted in UTF-16 code units (a supplementary-plane character counts 2).
R6 GUARD. If existing (the raw text, before R1) contains a BEGIN-MENTION anywhere — closed or not, inside a fence, a code
  span or mid-line — the result is none.
R7 SECTION BODY. Take the lines of section (R1) joined back with LF. If it contains a BEGIN-MARKER, drop everything up to
  and including the end of the first one (it may span lines). Then, if what remains contains an END-MARKER, drop the first one and everything
  after it. Trim WHITESPACE from both ends. Any other HTML comment (even one holding "-->") is ordinary text.
R8 SIGNATURE. Over the lines of the section body (split at LF only; R2-R4 apply): KEYS = the SET of the non-empty heading
  keys; if KEYS is empty -> none. The POOL = the SET of DISTINCT normalized (R5) forms of the body lines that are not blank
  (empty or all WHITESPACE), not headings, not fence lines (R2's per-line test, whatever the fence state), and whose
  normalized length exceeds 15. Content lines inside a fence are pool candidates, "#" lines inside a fence included; a
  one-line fence is not a fence line, so it is a candidate too (a closer of 16 or more backticks is one).
R9 RUNS. Take the headings of existing (R1-R3) in line order; a heading is MATCHED when its key is non-empty and in KEYS.
  A run is a maximal sequence of consecutive matched headings in that list (any levels; an unmatched heading, an
  empty-key heading included, ends it). Its range starts at the line of its first heading and ends (exclusive) at the
  line of the next heading of existing after the run, else at the line count of existing.
R10 MATCH. A run qualifies when at least 2 POOL entries each equal the normalized form of some line inside its range (any
  line there: prose, a heading, a fence line, a fenced line). Runs are examined in line order; the first that qualifies
  is the result (startLine, endLine = its range). No qualifying run -> none.
PINNED charset WHITESPACE = U+0009 U+000A U+000B U+000C U+000D U+0020 U+00A0 U+1680 U+2000-U+200A U+2028 U+2029 U+202F U+205F U+3000 U+FEFF CITED R1-R8 — the code's one whitespace set; U+0085 (NEL) is NOT in it, U+FEFF is
PINNED charset BREAK-CHAR = U+000D U+2028 U+2029 CITED R3 (c) — characters a heading's T may not contain past its leading whitespace
PINNED charset WORD-CHAR = ASCII A-Z a-z 0-9 "_" CITED BEGIN-MENTION BEGIN-MARKER — the word boundary after "begin"
PINNED grammar BEGIN-MENTION = "<!--" WHITESPACE* "rederive:begin" (end of text | a non-WORD-CHAR char) CITED R6 — nothing after it is required; the WHITESPACE run may span lines
PINNED grammar BEGIN-MARKER = BEGIN-MENTION's text, then the shortest stretch (any characters, LF included) up to the FIRST following "-->", provided no "<!--" starts inside that stretch CITED R7 — the leftmost "<!--" that completes it; a begin with no later "-->", or with a "<!--" before its first "-->", is not a marker at that "<!--" (a bare "-->", e.g. an arrow "A --> B", does close it)
PINNED grammar END-MARKER = "<!--" WHITESPACE* "rederive:end" WHITESPACE* "-->" CITED R7 — the whitespace runs may span lines
INVARIANT a non-none result has 0 <= startLine < endLine <= line count of existing, the line at startLine is a matched heading, and no heading of existing outside a fence lies strictly inside (startLine, endLine) unless it is matched.
ORACLE value -> oracles/adopt_find_duplicate.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 a450b3c9595e136f369dd440519b6f47d04360cc340a251d1bc1a6648eda855a
SCOPE IN: R1-R10 — findUnmanagedDuplicate and the helpers it calls. OUT: replaceUnmanagedBlock, extractKeptContent, the
  caller in cli/init.mts, file I/O.
